## REASONING

The user asks two things: (1) how peak SWE varies along the Wenatchee elevation gradient (mechanistic), and (2) how well ELM reproduces SNOTEL SWE (validation). Both hinge on the single candidate driver: elevation.

Here is the honest problem. Elevation controls peak SWE almost entirely through its effect on the *forcing* — air temperature (rain/snow partitioning via the freezing level) and orographic precipitation enhancement. ELM as a single-column model faithfully computes the snowpack energy/mass balance, but only if it is *fed* elevation-appropriate meteorology. The framework has no elevation-downscaling / lapse-rate capability for the forcing. If every column receives the same gridded forcing, then a set of columns "stratified by elevation" will differ only by soil/surface properties — which barely touch peak SWE — and will NOT reproduce the real elevation–SWE relationship. So the PRIMARY mechanism of the question is not representable as posed. This makes the mechanistic goal **infeasible without elevation-downscaled forcing**, and the overall verdict **partial**.

What the stack *can* do: run columns pinned to the 5 SNOTEL stations, and IF station-representative (elevation-appropriate) forcing is available at those points — either from the SNOTEL met itself or a downscaled product — validate simulated peak SWE and melt-out timing against observations. Absent downscaling, even the validation degrades to "how well does uniformly-forced ELM snow physics do," which is a much weaker test. I therefore record the downscaled-forcing dependency as the central backlog item and design the campaign so it becomes valid the moment that capability exists.

Sampling is expressed as RULES: elevation bands spanning 187–2870 m, concentrated around the rain-snow transition (~1200–1800 m) where peak SWE is most sensitive, plus validation columns pinned to the 5 SNOTEL stations. Snow spin-up and SWE output (H2OSNO/H2OSNO_TOP) are required.

